This page fixes the Lorentzian observer/current conventions, SI length-coordinate units, material state variables, and finite Killing-current normalization used in the thermodynamics–GR branch. Local material thermodynamics and nonnegative entropy-current divergence are distinct adopted physical premises. The finite-slab entropy identity is a conditional deduction from those premises and the exact draft mathematical balance supplier; the actual proof status and boundary-flux condition remain visible.

The diagram is a $1+1$ dimensional cross-section of the four-dimensional coordinate box. It marks the spacelike slice caps and the side boundary; the omitted spatial coordinates are suppressed. It makes no assertion about the causal character of the side faces and is not used in any proof.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.7) node[above] {$x^0=ct_{\rm phys}$};
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (5.2,0) node[right] {$x^1$};
  \draw[thick] (1,0.8) -- (4.2,0.8);
  \draw[thick] (1,2.8) -- (4.2,2.8);
  \draw[thick] (1,0.8) -- (1,2.8);
  \draw[thick] (4.2,0.8) -- (4.2,2.8);
  \node[left] at (1,0.8) {$\Sigma_{x^0_-}$};
  \node[left] at (1,2.8) {$\Sigma_{x^0_+}$};
  \node[right] at (4.2,1.8) {$B$};
  \node[below] at (2.6,0.4) {spatial interval};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

The example places two uncoupled two-level cells at different lapse values in one explicit Rindler stationary domain. It computes each finite Gibbs density, their tensor-product state, and the two proper-time KMS widths from the same Killing-time preparation. The picture shows constant-Rindler-position worldlines at $x=L$ and $x=2L$; the calculation uses the metric and finite matrices, not the sketch.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth]
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (4.4,0) node[right] {$x/L$};
  \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.5) node[above] {$x^0/L$};
  \draw[thick] (1,0) -- (1,3.1);
  \draw[thick] (2,0) -- (2,3.1);
  \fill (1,1.5) circle (2pt);
  \fill (2,2.2) circle (2pt);
  \node[below] at (1,0) {$x=L,\ N_1=1$};
  \node[below] at (2,0) {$x=2L,\ N_2=2$};
  \node[right] at (1,2.7) {$T_1=T_K$};
  \node[right] at (2,2.7) {$T_2=T_K/2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

This companion compares two proper accelerations in the specified massless Minkowski-vacuum rate model. The factor-of-two temperature relation and the common reference normalization are conditional model calculations; no detector apparatus or measured outcome is asserted.

The sketch uses length coordinates with $x^1$ horizontal and $x^0=ct$ vertical. Its shaded open wedge is $W_R=\{x^1>|x^0|\}$; the dashed rays are its null boundaries $x^0=\pm x^1$. The plotted branches are the right-wedge hyperbolae $\rho=\sqrt{(x^1)^2-(x^0)^2}$ at two positive radii. They illustrate the coordinate definitions only; the temperature and acceleration calculations use the explicit formulas in the item proof, not the drawing.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.78]
  \fill[blue!8] (0,0) -- (3.5,3.5) -- (4.2,3.5) -- (4.2,-3.5) -- (3.5,-3.5) -- cycle;
  \draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (4.35,0) node[right] {$x^1$};
  \draw[->] (0,-3.8) -- (0,3.8) node[above] {$x^0=ct$};
  \draw[dashed] (0,0) -- (3.65,3.65) node[pos=0.82,above left] {$x^0=x^1$};
  \draw[dashed] (0,0) -- (3.65,-3.65) node[pos=0.82,below left] {$x^0=-x^1$};
  \draw[thick,orange!85!black,domain=-1.25:1.25,samples=60,variable=\x]
    plot ({1.05*cosh(\x)},{1.05*sinh(\x)});
  \draw[thick,red!75!black,domain=-1.25:1.25,samples=60,variable=\x]
    plot ({1.75*cosh(\x)},{1.75*sinh(\x)});
  \node[orange!85!black] at (2.25,1.45) {$\rho_1$};
  \node[red!75!black] at (3.0,2.55) {$\rho_2>\rho_1$};
  \node[blue!50!black] at (3.85,0.45) {$W_R$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```
